WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.170
So yesterday CNN did this climate change forum.

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:05.860
They had back to back town halls.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:09.730
I think it went seven hours in total, each
candidate or many of the candidates.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:12.570
I don't have the full list of who even participated
in front of me.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:16.730
As I mentioned, I just dipped in and out of
a few minutes here, there and then read a

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:17.730
summary.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:19.769
Uh, the idea was let's go deep.

00:00:19.769 --> 00:00:23.349
Let's go deep with each candidate on climate
change.

00:00:23.349 --> 00:00:27.880
And one issue that keeps coming up time and
again and Elizabeth Warren handled that really

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:33.770
well, is this topic of plastic straws and
light bulbs and plastic straws and light bulbs

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:40.270
are a bogus distraction to the broader systemic
changes that need to happen in order to really

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:41.580
deal with the climate crisis.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:46.170
So there's a really good analogy here, and
it's undocumented immigration and wages.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:50.830
This has been waged effectively by huge corporations.

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:54.510
Huge corporations really are what keep wages
down.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:01:00.550
Huge corporations take advantage of undocumented
immigrant labor who is less likely to push

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:04.040
back against bad working conditions because
they know they're undocumented and they know

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:07.491
that they risk if they start reporting things
and getting on law enforcement's radar, they

00:01:07.491 --> 00:01:09.229
just risk deportation.

00:01:09.229 --> 00:01:15.030
So it is really corporations that have to
be blamed for perpetuating and even encouraging

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:17.479
the status quo around immigration.

00:01:17.479 --> 00:01:19.700
But corporations have done something genius.

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:25.609
They have fomented the idea that the middle
class in America and the lower middle class

00:01:25.609 --> 00:01:30.159
blue collar workers, they need to be at odds
with Latin American immigrants.

00:01:30.159 --> 00:01:34.880
They by fighting with each other are lowering
wages and blah blah, blah, blah blah.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:40.539
And it has worked brilliantly because very
infrequently do you seriously hear the corporate

00:01:40.539 --> 00:01:43.679
responsibility addressed when it comes to
keeping wages.

00:01:43.679 --> 00:01:47.609
You've got workers fighting workers and that's
exactly what corporations want.

00:01:47.609 --> 00:01:49.310
Apply that to climate change.

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:54.959
The building industry, the electric power
industry and the oil industry are some of

00:01:54.959 --> 00:02:00.009
the biggest polluters and biggest sources
of fossil fuel emissions.

00:02:00.009 --> 00:02:07.130
And yet they, through corporate PR campaigns
have brilliantly put the focus on have you

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:13.200
so switched off of plastic straws to the paper
straws or compostable potato starch straws

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.200
or whatever.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:19.769
Have you replaced all of your light bulbs
with the cfls which lasts however long it

00:02:19.769 --> 00:02:21.159
is or whatever.

00:02:21.159 --> 00:02:25.980
And Elizabeth Warren was asked about this
during her climate change town hall on CNN

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:28.150
and she brilliantly called it out.

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:32.750
But do you think that the government should
be in the business of telling you what kind

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:34.340
of light bulb you can have?

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:37.700
Oh, come on, give me a break.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:39.459
You know that?

00:02:39.459 --> 00:02:40.459
Yes.

00:02:40.459 --> 00:02:48.230
No, look, there are a lot of ways that we
try to change our energy consumption and our

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:51.909
pollution and God bless all of those ways.

00:02:51.909 --> 00:02:57.340
Some of it is with light bulbs, some of it
is on straws, some of it Dang is on cheeseburgers,

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:58.340
right?

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:00.520
There are a lot of different pieces to this.

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:05.230
And I get that people are trying to find the
part that they can work on and what can they

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:06.230
do?

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:08.379
And I'm in favor of that and I'm going to
help and I'm going to support.

00:03:08.379 --> 00:03:12.510
But understand this is exactly what the fossil
fuel industry helps we're all talking about.

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:15.870
That's what they want us to talk about.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:17.549
This is no problem.

00:03:17.549 --> 00:03:23.760
They want to be able to stir up a lot of controversy
around your light bulbs, around your straws

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:26.740
and around your cheeseburgers.

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:34.640
When 70% of the pollution of the carbon that
we're throwing into the air comes from three

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:44.299
industries and we can set our targets and
say by 2028 2030 and 2035 no more.

00:03:44.299 --> 00:03:46.090
Think about that right there.

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:48.519
Now the other 30% we still gotta work on

00:03:48.519 --> 00:03:50.060
this is the answer.

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:54.519
We can't let these bad actors do the sense-making.

00:03:54.519 --> 00:04:00.090
To borrow a term from earlier in the show,
something like 100 companies are responsible

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:03.349
for 71% of global emissions.

00:04:03.349 --> 00:04:09.749
Framing is extremely important when you look
at the discussion of Medicare for all we talk

00:04:09.749 --> 00:04:12.140
about the economic benefits.

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:16.750
We talk about the emotional, psychological
benefits of not having to worry about if I

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:19.680
get treatment or if I get sick, am I going
to go bankrupt?

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:23.270
There's all these intangible benefits to something
that is affordable.

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:28.310
We've seen it time and again it is affordable,
but you will hear this concern trolling of,

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:33.620
but, but if we do medicare for all, some people
will lose jobs.

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:40.840
Medical coders and insurance fraud investigators
and medical billing people, those are jobs

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:43.310
that should not have existed in the first
place.

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:45.419
I've addressed this issue already.

00:04:45.419 --> 00:04:49.889
Anytime government does anything, there is
a job impact.

00:04:49.889 --> 00:04:56.169
Even policies that create 10,000 jobs might
simultaneously destroy 500 meaning that the

00:04:56.169 --> 00:04:59.280
net benefit is only 9,500 jobs.

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:00.520
It's a red herring.

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:01.520
Okay?

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:08.370
It is the idea of putting pressure on people
to fight against each other for the benefit

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:12.750
of corporations and we can't be distracted
by this stuff.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:15.560
Fuel efficiency of cars is another one.

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:21.640
Listen, if you can go from cars on average
getting 10 miles per gallon to 60 miles per

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:25.190
gallon, I'm not pretending that that's not
going to have an impact.

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:32.250
But when you look at vehicles versus cargo
ships and aviation and all of this other stuff,

00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:38.690
it is again pressuring the consumer to believe
that they are the ones who need to solve the

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:44.440
problem by not using a plastic straw or by
getting a hybrid vehicle or for whatever the

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:45.440
case may be.

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:49.590
And the whole point of it is prevent demand
for corporate action.

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:55.440
And the way to do that is prevent demand for
government regulation of corporations, which

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:56.440
is the answer.

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:59.860
I mean that's just, that is the answer and
that's where we need to be looking.

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:06.530
That's why the narrative and agenda setting
power of corporate media is so important and

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:08.120
we can't let them do it.

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:09.310
Good for Elizabeth Warren for shutting it
down.

